{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>Fear HaShem, my son, and the king; with those who change do not mix.</b> (Proverbs 24:21)" "<b>Solomon</b> the king, peace be upon him, admonishes in this verse (Proverbs 24) that a person should fear the supernal King, may He be exalted, first, and after Him that he should fear the king on earth. And he equates them both together in the measure of fear, for he says: just as you fear the Holy One, blessed be He, who is present in every place, and you withdraw from sin out of fear of Him even though you do not see Him — so too must you fear the king, that you should withdraw of yourself from that for which you are liable to the death penalty out of fear of him, even though you do not stand before him. For it is impossible for a king of flesh and blood to be present in all places, and therefore you must have the king's dread and image engraved upon your heart in his absence from the place, and you should regard him as though you saw him with your eyes, just as you fear the supernal King, blessed be He, whom you have never seen with your eyes from the day you were born. <b>With those who change do not mix</b> — he admonishes not to change the matter, for there are those who transgress the will of HaShem out of fear of the king, and lo, they fear a man first and make fear of him the essence; and it is not fitting to do so, but rather the fitting thing is that HaShem be the beginning of his fear and the essence of his thought and all his actions. And since HaShem is King of the kings of kings, the Holy One, blessed be He, the first of all firsts, and the kingship of kings is from Him alone and His kingdom is eternal forever, it is not fitting at all to equate them. Therefore Scripture separated between them with the word 'my son' (בני), for it would have been fitting for the verse to say 'My son, fear HaShem and the king,' as is its practice in other verses that begin with the word בני; but he wished to separate between them, to explain that although the verse equated them in the matter of fear, their matter is not equal and one must not partner between them, for there is a great difference between them and a superiority, like the superiority of light over darkness. And the meaning of the verse is that fear of the king is the sustenance of the earth and a great necessity for the creatures, as our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded: 'Pray for the welfare of the government, for were it not for the fear of the government, a man would swallow his fellow alive.' But the great and mighty essence and the supreme excellence is to fear first the supernal King, blessed be He, upon whom the lives of all kings and their kingdom depend. From this Solomon said in the book of Ecclesiastes (8): ״אני פי מלך שמור ועל דברת שבועת אלהים״ — 'I: guard the king's mouth, and on account of the oath of Elohim.' He says: I say to you, 'guard the king's mouth' — that you should heed the king's mouth and do what he commands; and 'on account of the matters' — that you should keep the oath of Elohim, that one remember first the fear of HaShem and the oath that he swore at Sinai to accept upon himself the Torah and the commandments. And so they said in the Midrash: 'אני פי מלך שמור — I am prepared to heed the mouth of the kings of the nations who rule over us in taxes and duties and tolls; ועל דברת שבועת אלהים — but on account of the oath of Elohim, I do not heed the king's mouth.' For so we find with Hananiah, Mishael, and Azariah, who said to Nebuchadnezzar (Daniel 3:16): ״לא חשחין אנחנא על דנא פתגם להתבותך״. And the verse begins ״ענו שדרך מישך ועבד נגו״ and says: ״ואמרי למלכא נבוכדנצר לא חשחין״ and so forth. If 'the king,' why 'Nebuchadnezzar'? And if 'Nebuchadnezzar,' why 'the king'? Rather, this is what they said to him: 'You are a king over us in taxes and duties; but regarding this — that you tell us to worship idols — Nebuchadnezzar, you are not a king; you are like a dog.' That is to say, we are not permitted to come against the oath we swore at Mount Sinai; this is ״ועל דברת שבועת אלהים״. And know that the matter of the oath is a fence of fear, that a person should not swear by the Holy One, blessed be He, at all, even for the truth — how much more so for falsehood. And there was an incident with King Yannai, who had ten thousand towns in the King's Mountain, and all of them were destroyed on account of an oath of truth. How so? A man would say to his fellow: 'I swear that I am going to such-and-such a place, and I will eat and drink so-and-so,' and so with all their words, and they would fulfill their oaths — and all of them were destroyed. Now if one who swears for the truth is so treated, how much more so one who swears for falsehood! And how can a person be scrupulous in the quality of fear if he swears by the Holy One, blessed be He, for falsehood or even for the truth in vain? And because among all the transgressions there is none as severe as one who transgresses an oath, and the inclination of man's heart is evil from his youth, and he is constantly angry, and out of his anger he blurts out and swears, therefore the Torah needed to provide a remedy before the wound, and to command the annulment of vows before a qualified expert (yachid mumcheh) or before three laymen (hedyotot). And this is what is written:" "<b>And Moses spoke to the heads of the tribes of the children of Israel, saying: This is the thing which HaShem commanded.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): לבני ישראל — that they should say thus to the children of Israel. But our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded: ראשי המטות — 'the heads of the tribes,' alludes to the qualified expert (yachid mumcheh); לבני ישראל — 'of the children of Israel,' alludes to three laymen (hedyotot), just as monetary laws are before three laymen. And the reason this section was juxtaposed to the one above it, which is the section of the festivals, is because there He mentioned a 'high vow' and said (Numbers 29:39) ״לבד מנדריכם ונדבותיכם״; and this is the meaning of ״אשר צוה ה׳״ — 'which HaShem commanded' — for there He returns, to teach you that besides these vows mentioned there, there are also vows of a layman, which he is obligated to fulfill in his word. And there is another reason: just as the sanctity of the festivals is through a qualified expert, so too the annulment of vows is through a qualified expert."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 30, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:37:17.634228933Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}